 that in a linear hypergraph (where any two edges intersect in at most one vertex), there exists a matching of size at least n/(r(r-1)) where n is the number of vertices and r is the rank of the hypergraph. Wait, here the hypergraph is 3-uniform, so r=3. Then, according to some theorem, maybe the matching number is at least n/(r(r-1)) = 999/(3*2)= 999/6\u2248166.5. But we need 250. Hmm, so that doesn't reach our target either. \n\nWait, perhaps I recall that for linear hypergraphs, there are better bounds. Maybe something like n/(r + something). Let me check my memory. There is a theorem by Ajtai, Koml\u00f3s, Pintz, Spencer, and Szemer\u00e9di (AKS theorem) which gives lower bounds on the size of matchings in hypergraphs, but I might be mixing up different theorems. Alternatively, maybe using the Lov\u00e1sz local lemma to show that a matching of size 250 exists.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps considering that each academician has high degree (499) and using a greedy approach: since each academician is in many topics, we can select topics one by one, each time picking a topic and removing all topics that share an academician with it. Since each topic is connected to 3*498=1494 other topics, but when we pick a topic, we have to remove all topics that share any of the three academicians. Each academician is in 499 topics, so removing all 499 topics for each of the three academicians. But actually, when you pick a topic, you need to exclude all topics that include any of the three academicians. So, each topic is connected to 3*(499 -1) = 1494 topics. Wait, but in terms of the number of topics excluded when picking one topic: 3 academicians, each in 499 topics, but the original topic is counted three times. So, total excluded topics would be 3*499 - 3*1 (since the original topic is counted three times in the 3*499) which is 1497 -3=1494. So, each step we remove 1494 +1 =1495 topics (including the one we picked). \n\nIf we start with 166,167 topics, and each step removes 149