riple systems exist only for certain orders. For example, Kirkman's schoolgirl problem is about a resolvable Steiner triple system of order 15. In general, a necessary condition for resolvability is that v \u2261 3 mod 6. Here, 999 \u2261 3 mod 6, so it is possible. But resolvable Steiner triple systems (Kirkman systems) exist for all v \u2261 3 mod 6, but this is a result that was only proven in 2013 for the case when v \u2261 3 mod 6, v \u2260 6k + 3 where there are exceptions? Wait, maybe that's not exactly right. Let me recall: Ray-Chaudhuri and Wilson showed that resolvable designs exist for certain parameters. However, even if the Steiner triple system is resolvable, meaning it can be partitioned into parallel classes, each of 333 triples, then we could take one parallel class, which would be a perfect matching of 333 triples. But we need only 250. However, if the system isn't resolvable, this approach doesn't work. But maybe even if it isn't resolvable, there exists a large matching.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can use the fact that in any Steiner triple system, there exists a matching of size at least (v - 2)/3. Wait, for v=999, that would be (999 -2)/3\u2248997/3\u2248332.3, so 332. But that seems too good. Wait, if that's the case, then 332 would be possible, but we need only 250. However, I might be misremembering the theorem. Let me check.\n\nA theorem by R\u00f6dl, Ruci\u0144ski, and Szemer\u00e9di states that every Steiner triple system contains a matching covering all but at most o(v) vertices. So, asymptotically almost perfect. But that's a recent result. Alternatively, perhaps using a theorem by Wilson: in a Steiner triple system, the maximum matching size is at least v/3 - c for some constant c. But even so, if it's roughly v/3, which is 333, so then 250 is certainly possible. But how do we formalize this?\n\nAlternatively, think about the problem as a 3-partite hypergraph. Wait, but the hypergraph here is not necessarily 3-partite. However, if we c