ormula**:\n   From the summation, rearrange to isolate \\((xy + yz + zx)\\):\n   \\[\n   2(x^2 + y^2 + z^2) + (xy + yz + zx) = 326\n   \\]\n   Let's substitute \\(2(x^2 + y^2 + z^2) = 326 - (xy + yz + zx)\\):\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{56}\n   \\]\n\nThis is the final answer. The value of the expression \\(xy + yz + zx\\) is \\( \\boxed{56} \\).", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Two ships are traveling in the sea on perpendicular courses such that both must pass through a fixed point O (each at its own time). After passing point O, the ships will continue their straight-line movement without stopping. The speeds of the ships are equal and constant. At noon, the ships have not yet passed point O and are located 20 km and 15 km from it, respectively. Will the ships be within each other's visibility range at any point if the visibility on that day is 4 km?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so there's this problem about two ships traveling on perpendicular courses, both passing through point O but at different times. They have the same speed, and we need to figure out if they'll ever be within 4 km of each other after they pass point O. Let me try to break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let me visualize the scenario. Both ships are approaching point O from different directions, perpendicular to each other. Ship A is 20 km away from O at noon, and Ship B is 15 km away from O at the same time. Since their speeds are equal, the time each takes to reach O will depend on their distances. Ship A is farther away, so it should take longer to reach O than Ship B. After passing O, they continue moving in straight lines. The question is whether at any point after passing O, their distance from each other will be less than 4 km.\n\nWait, actually, the problem states that both ships have not yet passed point O at noon. So at noon, Ship A is 20 km away from O, approaching it, and Ship B is 15 km away from O, also approaching