3b8 cos\u03b8 + 8 cos2\u03b8 sin\u03b8 - 6 sin2\u03b8 = 0\n\nFactor sin2\u03b8 from first and third terms:\n\nsin2\u03b8 (8 cos\u03b8 - 6) + 8 cos2\u03b8 sin\u03b8 = 0\n\nHmm, not sure if that's helpful. Maybe express everything in terms of sin\u03b8 and cos\u03b8. Since sin2\u03b8 = 2 sin\u03b8 cos\u03b8, let's substitute that:\n\n8 [2 sin\u03b8 cos\u03b8 * cos\u03b8 + cos2\u03b8 sin\u03b8] = 6 * 2 sin\u03b8 cos\u03b8\n\nSimplify left side:\n\n8 [2 sin\u03b8 cos\u00b2\u03b8 + cos2\u03b8 sin\u03b8] = 12 sin\u03b8 cos\u03b8\n\nFactor sin\u03b8:\n\n8 sin\u03b8 [2 cos\u00b2\u03b8 + cos2\u03b8] = 12 sin\u03b8 cos\u03b8\n\nAssuming sin\u03b8 \u2260 0 (since \u03b8 is an angle in a triangle, it can't be 0), we can divide both sides by sin\u03b8:\n\n8 [2 cos\u00b2\u03b8 + cos2\u03b8] = 12 cos\u03b8\n\nNow, let's recall that cos2\u03b8 = 2 cos\u00b2\u03b8 - 1. Substitute that in:\n\n8 [2 cos\u00b2\u03b8 + 2 cos\u00b2\u03b8 - 1] = 12 cos\u03b8\n\nSimplify inside the brackets:\n\n8 [4 cos\u00b2\u03b8 - 1] = 12 cos\u03b8\n\nLeft side becomes 32 cos\u00b2\u03b8 - 8 = 12 cos\u03b8\n\nBring all terms to left side:\n\n32 cos\u00b2\u03b8 - 12 cos\u03b8 - 8 = 0\n\nDivide all terms by 4 to simplify:\n\n8 cos\u00b2\u03b8 - 3 cos\u03b8 - 2 = 0\n\nSo, we have a quadratic equation in terms of cos\u03b8. Let's set x = cos\u03b8:\n\n8x\u00b2 - 3x - 2 = 0\n\nSolving this quadratic equation:\n\nx = [3 \u00b1 sqrt(9 + 64)] / 16 = [3 \u00b1 sqrt(73)] / 16\n\nWait, discriminant is b\u00b2 - 4ac = 9 + 64 = 73. So sqrt(73) is irrational. But since cos\u03b8 must be between -1 and 1, and \u03b8 is an acute angle (as angle C in a triangle where angle A is 2\u03b8 and angle B is 180 - 3\u03b8; all angles must be positive, so 3\u03b8 < 180\u00b0, so \u03b8 < 60\u00b0, so cos\u03b8 is positive and greater than 0.5, since cos60\u00b0 = 0.5 and \u03b8 < 60\u00b0.\n\nSo cos\u03b8 = [3 + sqrt(73)] / 16 or [3 - sqrt(73)] / 16. Let's compute both:\n\nFirst solution: [3 + sqrt(73)] / 16 \u2248 [3 + 8.544] / 