ia:6. 2 infected.\n\nt=2: 2 infected release 4 viruses, die. Remaining 4 divide into 8. Total bacteria:8. 4 infected.\n\nt=3:4 infected release 8 viruses, die. Remaining 4 divide into 8. Total bacteria:8. 8 infected.\n\nt=4:8 infected release 16 viruses, die. Remaining 0. Eradicated at t=4.\n\nWait, n=4 takes 4 minutes. So the time seems to be equal to n?\n\nWait, for n=1, t=1. n=2, t=2. n=3, t=3. n=4, t=4. So is the time equal to n minutes?\n\nWait, but let me check n=4 again.\n\nt=0:4 bacteria, 1 infected.\n\nt=1: infected dies, releases 2 viruses. 3 uninfected become 6. Total bacteria 6. 2 get infected.\n\nt=2: 2 infected die, release 4 viruses. 6-2=4 uninfected become 8. Total bacteria 8. 4 get infected.\n\nt=3:4 infected die, release 8 viruses. 8-4=4 uninfected become 8. Total bacteria 8. 8 infected.\n\nt=4:8 infected die, release 16 viruses. 0 uninfected. Total bacteria 0. Eradicated.\n\nYes, that's 4 minutes for n=4. So seems like time t equals n.\n\nBut wait, n=1:1 minute, n=2:2 minutes, etc. So maybe in general, the time to eradication is n minutes.\n\nBut wait, let's check n=5.\n\nt=0:5 bacteria, 1 infected.\n\nt=1: releases 2 viruses, 4 uninfected become 8. Total 8. 2 infected.\n\nt=2:2 release 4 viruses, 6 uninfected become 12. Total 12. 4 infected.\n\nt=3:4 release 8 viruses, 8 uninfected become 16. Total 16.8 infected.\n\nt=4:8 release 16 viruses, 8 uninfected become 16. Total 16.16 infected.\n\nt=5:16 release 32 viruses, 0 uninfected. Total bacteria 0. Eradicated at t=5.\n\nYes, so n=5 takes 5 minutes. So it seems that the time required is n minutes.\n\nBut let's see why this happens. Each minute, the number of infected bacteria doubles, but the uninfected bacteria also double each minute. However, the number of viruses released is equal to 2 times the number of infected bacteria, which allows the number of infected bacteria to double each time.\n\nWait, but how does the number of infected bacteria progress?\n\nAt each step, the number of infected bacteria is equal to the number of viruses released in the previous step. Since each infected bacterium releases 2 viruses, the number of viruses is 2 * previous infected bacteria. Then, each virus infects a bacterium, so the number of infected bacteria next step is equal to the number of viruses, which is 2 * previous infected. So actually, the number of infected bacteria doubles each step. Wait, but in our previous examples, the number of infected bacteria was doubling each step. For n=2:\n\nt=0:1 infected.\n\nt=1:2 infected.\n\nt=2:4 infected, but all bacteria are infected, so maybe they get eradicated.\n\nWait, but in reality, when you have more viruses than bacteria, all bacteria get infected. So maybe the number of infected bacteria at time t is min(2^t, remaining bacteria).\n\nBut in the case of n=2:\n\nt=0:1 infected.\n\nt=1:2 infected (since viruses=2, bacteria=2).\n\nt=2: all infected, but bacteria=2, so viruses=4, but only 2 bacteria. Wait, no, after t=1, bacteria=2, which are all infected. Then at t=2, those 2 infected bacteria release 4 viruses, and die. Then, the remaining bacteria are 0, since all were infected. So the infection process stops here.\n\nBut in the case of n=3:\n\nt=0:1 infected.\n\nt=1:2 infected (viruses=2, bacteria=4). Wait, wait, no. At t=1, original infected dies, viruses=2. The other 2 bacteria become 4. So total bacteria=4. Then viruses=2 infect 2 bacteria. So infected=2.\n\nt=2: those 2 infected die, release 4 viruses. The remaining 2 bacteria become 4. Total bacteria=4. Viruses=4 infect 4 bacteria.\n\nt=3:4 infected die, release 8 viruses. Remaining bacteria 0. So done in 3 minutes.\n\nSo the number of infected bacteria doubles each time: 1, 2, 4, 8,... until it surpasses the number of available bacteria, which are also doubling each time (except for the ones being infected).\n\nWait, so the number of bacteria after each minute is:\n\nAfter infection and division, the number of bacteria is 2*(previous total - previous infected). Because previous infected die, and the remaining (previous total - previous infected) bacteria each divide into 2.\n\nWait, let's formalize this.\n\nLet B(t) be the number of bacteria at time t.\n\nLet I(t) be the number of infected bacteria at time t.\n\nAt t=0: B(0)=n, I(0)=1.\n\nAt each time step t+1:\n\n- The I(t) infected bacteria release 2*I(t) viruses and die.\n\n- The remaining bacteria B(t) - I(t) divide into 2*(B(t) - I(t)).\n\n- So the new number of bacteria B(t+1) = 2*(B(t) - I(t)).\n\n- The number of viruses released is 2*I(t), which then infect min(2*I(t), B(t+1)) bacteria, so I(t+1) = min(2*I(t), B(t+1)).\n\nBut in our examples, the infection is always such that 2*I(t) <= B(t+1). Let's check.\n\nFor n=2:\n\nt=0: B=2, I=1.\n\nB(1)=2*(2 -1)=2. Viruses=2*1=2. So I(1)=2.\n\nt=1: B=2, I=2.\n\nB(2)=2*(2 -2)=0. Viruses=4. But no bacteria left. So done at t=2.\n\nSimilarly, n=3:\n\nt=0: B=3, I=1.\n\nB(1)=2*(3 -1)=4. Viruses=2. I(1)=2.\n\nt=1: B=4, I=2.\n\nB(2)=2*(4 -2)=4. Viruses=4. I(2)=4.\n\nt=2: B=4, I=4.\n\nB(3)=2*(4 -4)=0. Eradicated at t=3.\n\nWait, so in these cases, B(t+1) = 2*(B(t) - I(t)), and I(t+1) = 2*I(t) only if 2*I(t) <= B(t+1). But in the cases above, 2*I(t) was equal to B(t+1):\n\nFor n=3:\n\nAt t=0, I=1. B(1)=4. 2*I(0)=2 <=4. So I(1)=2.\n\nAt t=1, I=2. B(2)=4. 2*I(1)=4 <=4. So I(2)=4.\n\nAt t=2, I=4. B(3)=0. So I(3)=0.\n\nSimilarly, for n=4:\n\nt=0: B=4, I=1.\n\nB(1)=2*(4 -1)=6. Viruses=2. I(1)=2.\n\nt=1: B=6, I=2.\n\nB(2)=2*(6 -2)=8. Viruses=4. I(2)=4.\n\nt=2: B=8, I=4.\n\nB(3)=2*(8 -4)=8. Viruses=8. I(3)=8.\n\nt=3: B=8, I=8.\n\nB(4)=0. Eradicated at t=4.\n\nSo here, 2*I(t) is equal to B(t+1) each time until the end.\n\nWait, so in this case, the number of infected bacteria is doubling each time until they can infect all the remaining bacteria. Then, once all bacteria are infected, they die off in the next step.\n\nSo this suggests that the number of infected bacteria follows I(t) = 2^t, but constrained by the number of bacteria available.\n\nBut B(t) follows B(t) = 2*(B(t-1) - I(t-1)).\n\nSo starting from B(0)=n, I(0)=1.\n\nLet me try to model this.\n\nAt each step:\n\nB(t+1) = 2*(B(t) - I(t))\n\nI(t+1) = min(2*I(t), B(t+1))\n\nBut in our examples, after the first few steps, I(t+1)=2*I(t) because B(t+1) >=2*I(t). Then, at some point, B(t+1)=2*(B(t) - I(t)) becomes equal to 2*I(t). Let's see:\n\nSuppose that at some step t, I(t) = 2^t. Then B(t) is following some pattern.\n\nWait, perhaps there's a pattern here where B(t) = 2^t*(n - (2^t -1)). Let me check with n=2:\n\nAt t=0: B(0)=2 =2^0*(2 - (2^0 -1))=1*(2 -0)=2. Correct.\n\nt=1: B(1)=2*(2 -1)=2. Which is 2^1*(2 - (2^1 -1))=2*(2 -1)=2. Correct.\n\nt=2: B(2)=2*(2 -2)=0. Which is 2^2*(2 - (2^2 -1))=4*(2 -3)=4*(-1)=-4. Doesn't match. Hmm, maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, let's look for a recursive formula.\n\nWe have B(t+1) = 2*(B(t) - I(t)), and I(t+1)=2*I(t) if possible.\n\nIf we suppose that I(t) = 2^t, then:\n\nB(t+1) = 2*(B(t) - 2^t)\n\nLet's solve this recurrence.\n\nAssuming I(t)=2^t, then:\n\nB(t) = 2*(B(t-1) - 2^{t-1})\n\nLet's expand this:\n\nB(0)=n\n\nB(1)=2*(n -1)\n\nB(2)=2*(B(1) -2)=2*(2n -2 -2)=2*(2n -4)=4n -8\n\nB(3)=2*(B(2) -4)=2*(4n -8 -4)=8n -24\n\nB(4)=2*(B(3) -8)=2*(8n -24 -8)=16n -64\n\nHmm, seems like B(t) = 2^t *n - 2^{t}*(2^t -1)\n\nWait, let's see:\n\nAt t=1: 2n - 2*(1) =2n -2. But B(1)=2n -2. Wait, no:\n\nWait, 2^t *n - 2^{t}*(2^t -1) =2^t(n - (2^t -1)). For t=1:2(n -1), which matches B(1)=2(n-1).\n\nt=2:4(n -3). But B(2)=4n -8. Which is 4n -8=4(n -2). But according to the formula, it would be 4(n -3). Not matching.\n\nWait, maybe another approach.\n\nLet\u2019s try to find a closed-form expression for B(t).\n\nThe recurrence is B(t) = 2*(B(t-1) - I(t-1)).\n\nAssuming I(t-1)=2^{t-1}, then B(t) =2*(B(t-1) -2^{t-1}).\n\nLet\u2019s write down the terms:\n\nB(0)=n\n\nB(1)=2(n -1)\n\nB(2)=2(B(1)-2)=2(2n -2 -2)=2(2n -4)=4n -8\n\nB(3)=2(B(2)-4)=2(4n -8 -4)=8n -24\n\nB(4)=2(B(3)-8)=2(8n -24 -8)=16n -64\n\nB(5)=2(B(4)-16)=2(16n -64 -16)=32n -160\n\nObserving these terms:\n\nAt t=1: 2n -2=2n -2*1\n\nt=2:4n -8=4n -2*4\n\nt=3:8n -24=8n -2*12\n\nt=4:16n -64=16n -2*32\n\nt=5:32n -160=32n -2*80\n\nHmm, the coefficients of n are 2^t, and the subtracted terms are 2 multiplied by 1,4,12,32,80,...\n\nLooking at the subtracted terms:1,4,12,32,80,...\n\nLet's see the pattern:\n\n1=1\n\n4=1*4\n\n12=3*4\n\n32=8*4\n\n80=20*4\n\nWait, perhaps the subtracted term is 2^{t} * t /2 ?\n\nWait, let's check:\n\nFor t=1: 2*1*(1)/2=1. But subtracted term is 2*1=2. Not matching.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the subtracted term is 2^{t}*(t). For t=1:2*1=2, but subtracted term is 2*1=2. t=1: subtracted term 2. t=2: subtracted term 8=2*4. Wait, no. Original subtracted terms:\n\nAt t=1: B(1)=2n -2. So subtracted term is 2.\n\nAt t=2: B(2)=4n -8. Subtracted 8.\n\nt=3:8n -24. Subtracted 24.\n\nt=4:16n -64. Subtracted 64.\n\nt=5:32n -160. Subtracted 160.\n\nLooking at subtracted terms:2,8,24,64,160,...\n\nDivided by 2:1,4,12,32,80,...\n\nThis sequence is 1,4,12,32,80,...\n\nLooking up the OEIS, this might be similar to A001787, which is 0,1,4,12,32,80,... which is n*2^{n-1}. But here starting from n=1:1=1*2^{0}, 4=2*2^{1},12=3*2^{2},32=4*2^{3},80=5*2^{4}, etc. So yes, the subtracted term after t minutes is t*2^{t-1} multiplied by 2. Wait:\n\nOriginal subtracted terms are 2,8,24,64,160,...\n\nWhich is 2=2*1, 8=2*4,24=2*12,64=2*32,160=2*80. So each subtracted term is 2*(t*2^{t-1})=t*2^{t}.\n\nSo general formula for B(t):\n\nB(t)=2^{t}n - t*2^{t}\n\n=2^{t}(n -t)\n\nWait, let's check:\n\nFor t=1:2(n -1). Correct.\n\nt=2:4(n -2). But wait, B(2)=4n -8=4(n -2). Correct.\n\nt=3:8(n -3). But original B(3)=8n -24=8(n -3). Correct.\n\nt=4:16(n -4)=16n -64. Correct.\n\nYes! So general formula: B(t)=2^{t}(n -t).\n\nWow, that's a neat formula. So after t minutes, the number of bacteria is 2^t(n -t). And the number of infected bacteria is I(t)=2^{t}.\n\nBut this formula holds as long as I(t) <= B(t). Wait, but according to our earlier examples, when does the infection overtake the bacteria?\n\nWait, because I(t)=2^t, and B(t)=2^t(n -t). So as long as 2^t <=2^t(n -t), i.e., n -t >=1. So when n -t >=1, which is t <=n -1. Then, at t =n, B(n)=2^n(n -n)=0.\n\nSo if the process continues until t=n, then B(n)=0. Hence, the culture is eradicated at t=n minutes.\n\nBut we need to verify this.\n\nWait, let's take n=2. B(t)=2^t(2 -t). So at t=2:2^2(2 -2)=0. Correct.\n\nFor n=3, B(3)=8*(3-3)=0. Correct.\n\nSo yes, the formula suggests that after t=n minutes, the number of bacteria is zero.\n\nBut how does the infection process go? Because each time, I(t)=2^t, and B(t)=2^t(n -t). So the number of infected bacteria is I(t)=2^t, and the number of uninfected bacteria is B(t) - I(t)=2^t(n -t) -2^t=2^t(n -t -1). Wait, but in the recurrence, B(t+1)=2*(B(t) -I(t)).\n\nIf B(t)=2^t(n -t), then B(t) -I(t)=2^t(n -t) -2^t=2^t(n -t -1). Then B(t+1)=2*(2^t(n -t -1))=2^{t+1}(n -t -1)=2^{t+1}(n -(t+1)). Which matches the formula. So the formula holds.\n\nTherefore, as long as I(t)=2^t <= B(t)=2^t(n -t), which simplifies to n -t >=1, which is t <=n -1. So for t from 0 to n-1, I(t) <= B(t). Then, at t=n, B(n)=0, as per the formula.\n\nBut how does the infection proceed? For each t, from 0 to n-1, the number of infected bacteria is I(t)=2^t. At each step, those infected bacteria release 2*I(t)=2^{t+1} viruses. Then, the remaining bacteria B(t) -I(t)=2^t(n -t) -2^t=2^t(n -t -1) divide into 2*(B(t) -I(t))=2^{t+1}(n -t -1)=B(t+1). Then, the 2^{t+1} viruses infect min(2^{t+1}, B(t+1)) bacteria. Since B(t+1)=2^{t+1}(n - (t+1)). For t+1 <=n -1, which is t<=n-2, B(t+1)=2^{t+1}(n - (t+1)) >=2^{t+1} because n - (t+1)>=1. So the number of infected bacteria at t+1 is I(t+1)=2^{t+1}. This continues until t+1=n -1, where B(n -1)=2^{n -1}(n - (n -1))=2^{n -1}(1)=2^{n -1}, and I(n -1)=2^{n -1}. So at t=n -1, all bacteria are infected. Then, at t=n, those infected bacteria release 2*I(n -1)=2^n viruses and die, leaving B(n)=0.\n\nTherefore, the culture is eradicated at t=n minutes.\n\nThis seems to hold for the examples we tried: n=1 eradicated at t=1, n=2 at t=2, etc.\n\nTherefore, the general answer is that the bacterial culture will be eradicated after n minutes.\n\nBut let me check n=1 case specifically:\n\nAt t=0: B=1, I=1.\n\nAt t=1: B(1)=2^1(1 -1)=0. So done in 1 minute. Correct.\n\nAnother check for n=5:\n\nB(t)=2^t(5 -t). At t=5:32*(5-5)=0. So yes, eradicated at t=5.\n\nTherefore, the time it takes is n minutes.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe bacterial culture will be eradicated after \\boxed{n} minutes.", "deepseek_solution": "To determine whether the bacterial culture will be eradicated and the time it will take, we analyze the process step by minute. \n\n1. **Initial Setup**:\n   - At time \\( t = 0 \\), there are \\( n \\) bacteria, one of which is infected.\n\n2. **Infection and Division Process**:\n   - Each minute, the infected bacteria release 2 viruses and die.\n   - During the same minute, all uninfected bacteria divide once.\n   - The released viruses infect new bacteria, with each virus infecting one bacterium.\n\n3. **Recurrence Relations**:\n   - Let \\( B(t) \\) be the number of bacteria at time \\( t \\).\n   - Let \\( I(t) \\) be the number of infected bacteria at time \\( t \\).\n   - The recurrence relations are:\n     \\[\n     B(t+1) = 2 \\left( B(t) - I(t) \\right)\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     I(t+1) = \\min(2I(t), B(t+1))\n     \\]\n\n4. **Pattern Analysis**:\n   - Through analysis, it is found that the number of infected bacteria \\( I(t) \\) doubles each minute until it can infect all remaining bacteria.\n   - The number of bacteria \\( B(t) \\) follows the formula \\( B(t) = 2^t (n - t) \\).\n\n5. **Conclusion**:\n   - The number of bacteria \\( B(t) \\) becomes zero when \\( t = n \\). At this point, all bacteria have been infected and subsequently died, leaving no bacteria remaining.\n\nThus, the bacterial culture will be eradicated after \\(\\boxed{n}\\) minutes.", "ground_truth_solution": "To determine if the bacterial culture will be completely wiped out by the viruses, and if so, how long it will take, follow the steps below:\n\n1. **Initial Conditions:**\n   - Let the initial number of bacteria be \\( n \\).\n   - A single virus infects one bacterium at time \\( t = 0 \\).\n\n2. **Minute-by-Minute Evolution:**\n   - At \\( t = 1 \\) minute, the infected bacterium bursts, releasing 2 new viruses. Concurrently, all other \\( n-1 \\) non-infected bacteria divide into \\( 2(n-1) \\) bacteria.\n   - Each minute \\( t = k \\), the number of new viruses doubles the number of infected bacteria from the previous minute.\n\n3. **General Case:**\n   - Denote by \\( k \\)-type bacteria those that are infected at time \\( t = k \\).\n   - Let\u2019s assume that if there are uninfected bacteria at minute \\( k \\), by the end of this minute, each bacterium of type \\( k-1 \\) will have released 2 viruses, promptly infecting 2 more bacteria.\n\n4. **Number of Infected Bacteria each Minute:**\n   - At time \\( t = k \\), there are \\( 2^k \\) viruses released since each \\( k \\)-type bacterium splits into 2 viruses.\n   - Therefore, by the end of minute \\( k \\), there will be \\( 2(k-1) \\)-type bacteria produced from previously infected bacteria.\n\n5. **Counting Bacteria and Viruses:**\n   - If there are still uninfected bacteria at minute \\( m \\), then by the end, all \\( k < m \\) types will have duplicated. Hence, every \\( m \\)-type bacterium will have \\( 2^{m-k} \\) descendants.\n\n6. **Number of Bacteria at Minute \\( m \\):**\n   - The total number of non-infected bacteria at minute \\( m \\) is \\( (n - m) \\cdot 2^m \\).\n   - The number of type $k$ bacteria duplicating \\( m-k \\) times results in \\( 2^{m-k} \\) descendants.\n\n7. **Bacterial Extinction:**\n   - Following this doubling process, for general \\( k \\), the total number of bacteria becomes \\( n \\cdot 2^k \\) by minute \\( k \\).\n   - At the specific minute \\( m = n - 1 \\), the number of \\( k \\)-type bacteria that will infect exactly \\( 2^{n-1} \\) more bacteria results in \\( (n-1) \\cdot 2^{n-1} \\) being infected and subsequently starving non-infected bacteria.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nBy minute \\( n-1 \\), the entire population of bacteria, \\( 2^{n-1} \\) in count for the infected as well as non-infected, reaches an infected state. Therefore, the bacterial culture will be wiped out by the end of the \\( n \\)-th minute.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{n}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) be positive numbers such that \\( a + b = 1 \\). Show that \\(\\left( a + \\frac{1}{a} \\right)^2 + \\left( b + \\frac{1}{b} \\right)^2 \\geq \\frac{25}{2} \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to show that for positive numbers a and b where a + b = 1, the expression (a + 1/a)\u00b2 + (b + 1/b)\u00b2 is greater than or equal to 25/2. Hmm, let me think about how to approach this.\n\nFirst, since a and b are positive and their sum is 1, they must each be between 0 and 1. That means 1/a and 1/b are both greater than 1, right? Because if a is less than 1, 1/a is greater than 1. So, each term (a + 1/a) and (b + 1/b) is a number greater than 1 plus something else. Squaring those terms will likely make the expression pretty large. But how do I find the minimum value?\n\nMaybe I can use calculus here. If I can express the function in terms of one variable, take its derivative, find critical points, and then determine the minimum. Let's try that.\n\nSince a + b = 1, I can substitute b = 1 - a. Then the expression becomes:\n\n[(a + 1/a)\u00b2 + ( (1 - a) + 1/(1 - a) )\u00b2]\n\nLet me define this as a function f(a):\n\nf(a) = (a + 1/a)\u00b2 + (1 - a + 1/(1 - a))\u00b2\n\nNow, I need to find the minimum of f(a) for a in (0,1). To do this, I can take the derivative f\u2019(a), set it equal to zero, and solve for a. The critical points will give me potential minima or maxima.\n\nBut before jumping into calculus, maybe there's a simpler algebraic approach using inequalities like Cauchy-Schwarz or AM-GM. Let me recall those.\n\nThe Cauchy-Schwarz inequality states that for vectors u and v, (u\u00b7v)\u00b2 \u2264 (u\u00b7u)(v\u00b7v). Not sure if that's directly applicable here.\n\nWhat about AM-GM? The Arithmetic Mean - Geometric Mean inequality says that for non-negative numbers, the arithmetic mean is greater than or equal to the geometric mean. But again, not sure how to apply that here immediately.\n\nAlternatively, maybe expanding the squares and simplifying the expression. Let's try expanding the terms:\n\n(a + 1/a)\u00b2 = a\u00b2 + 2*(a)*(1/a) + (1/a)\u00b2 = a\u00b2 + 2 + 1/a\u00b2\n\nSimilarly, (b + 1/b)\u00b2 = b\u00b2 + 2 + 1/b\u00b2\n\nSo, the entire expression is (a\u00b2 + 2 + 1/a\u00b2) + (b\u00b2 + 2 + 1/b\u00b2) = a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 + 4 + 1/a\u00b2 + 1/b\u00b2\n\nTherefore, the expression simplifies to:\n\n(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2) + (1/a\u00b2 + 1/b\u00b2) + 4\n\nSince a + b = 1, maybe we can express a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 in terms of (a + b)\u00b2 - 2ab. Let's see:\n\na\u00b2 + b\u00b2 = (a + b)\u00b2 - 2ab = 1 - 2ab\n\nSo substituting back, the expression becomes:\n\n(1 - 2ab) + (1/a\u00b2 + 1/b\u00b2) + 4 = 5 - 2ab + 1/a\u00b2 + 1/b\u00b2\n\nHmm, so now the problem reduces to showing that 5 - 2ab + 1/a\u00b2 + 1/b\u00b2 >= 25/2\n\nSubtracting 5 from both sides, this is equivalent to:\n\n-2ab + 1/a\u00b2 + 1/b\u00b2 >= 25/2 - 5 = 15/2\n\nSo, need to show that 1/a\u00b2 + 1/b\u00b2 - 2ab >= 15/2\n\nBut not sure if this is helpful. Maybe another approach. Let's see.\n\nAlternatively, since a and b are positive with a + b = 1, perhaps using the method of Lagrange multipliers. But that might be overkill for this problem. Let me see.\n\nWait, maybe symmetry can help here. If a = b = 1/2, since a + b =1, then substituting into the original expression:\n\n(a + 1/a)^2 + (b + 1/b)^2 = (1/2 + 2)^2 + (1/2 + 2)^2 = (5/2)^2 + (5/2)^2 = 25/4 + 25/4 = 25/2\n\nSo at a = b = 1/2, the expression equals 25/2. The problem is to show that this is the minimum. Therefore, the expression is minimized when a = b = 1/2, and the minimum value is 25/2.\n\nBut to confirm that this is indeed the minimum, we need to check if the function is convex or concave, or use some inequality to show that deviation from a = b = 1/2 would increase the value.\n\nAlternatively, using the method of derivatives. Let's try that now.\n\nLet me define f(a) = (a + 1/a)^2 + (1 - a + 1/(1 - a))^2\n\nCompute f'(a):\n\nFirst, let's compute derivative of (a + 1/a)^2:\n\nLet u = a + 1/a, so u\u2019 = 1 - 1/a\u00b2\n\nThen d/da [u\u00b2] = 2u * u\u2019 = 2*(a + 1/a)*(1 - 1/a\u00b2)\n\nSimilarly, for the term (1 - a + 1/(1 - a))^2:\n\nLet v = (1 - a) + 1/(1 - a), so dv/da = -1 + [1/(1 - a)^2]\n\nThus, derivative of v\u00b2 is 2v*(-1 + 1/(1 - a)^2)\n\nTherefore, f\u2019(a) = 2*(a + 1/a)*(1 - 1/a\u00b2) + 2*( (1 - a) + 1/(1 - a) )*(-1 + 1/(1 - a)^2 )\n\nSet derivative equal to zero:\n\n2*(a + 1/a)*(1 - 1/a\u00b2) + 2*( (1 - a) + 1/(1 - a) )*(-1 + 1/(1 - a)^2 ) = 0\n\nDivide both sides by 2:\n\n(a + 1/a)*(1 - 1/a\u00b2) + ( (1 - a) + 1/(1 - a) )*(-1 + 1/(1 - a)^2 ) = 0\n\nThis equation looks complicated, but maybe if we assume that a = b = 1/2 is the critical point, we can check:\n\nLet a = 1/2, then b = 1 - 1/2 = 1/2\n\nCompute each term:\n\nFirst term: (1/2 + 2)*(1 - 4) = (5/2)*(-3) = -15/2\n\nSecond term: (1/2 + 2)*(-1 + 4) = (5/2)*(3) = 15/2\n\nAdding them: -15/2 + 15/2 = 0. So yes, a = 1/2 is a critical point.\n\nNow, we need to check if this is a minimum. Let's compute the second derivative or test around the point.\n\nAlternatively, consider the symmetry. If a = b = 1/2 gives the minimal value, then perhaps the function is symmetric and convex around this point. Let's test with another value.\n\nTake a = 1/3, then b = 2/3\n\nCompute the expression:\n\n(a + 1/a)^2 + (b + 1/b)^2 = (1/3 + 3)^2 + (2/3 + 3/2)^2\n\nCompute each term:\n\n1/3 + 3 = 10/3, squared is 100/9 \u2248 11.111\n\n2/3 + 3/2 = 4/6 + 9/6 = 13/6, squared is 169/36 \u2248 4.694\n\nTotal \u2248 11.111 + 4.694 \u2248 15.805, which is approximately 15.805. But 25/2 = 12.5, which is less. Wait, that can't be. Wait, hold on, 100/9 + 169/36 = (400 + 169)/36 = 569/36 \u2248 15.805, which is greater than 25/2 \u2248 12.5. So this is consistent with the minimum being 25/2 at a = b = 1/2.\n\nWait, but 15.8 is greater than 12.5, so that's okay. Let's check another point. Let a approach 0. Let's take a = 0.1, b = 0.9\n\nCompute (0.1 + 10)^2 + (0.9 + 1/0.9)^2\n\nFirst term: 10.1 squared \u2248 102.01\n\nSecond term: 0.9 + approx 1.111 \u2248 2.011, squared \u2248 4.044\n\nTotal \u2248 102.01 + 4.044 \u2248 106.054, which is way larger. So as a approaches 0, the expression tends to infinity. Similarly, when a approaches 1, same thing. So the function has a minimum somewhere in between. Since at a = 1/2, the value is 25/2 = 12.5, and when moving away from 1/2, the value increases. Therefore, 25/2 is indeed the minimum.\n\nBut to formally prove this, not just test points, we need a more rigorous approach.\n\nAlternative approach: Use convexity.\n\nCheck if the function f(a) is convex on (0,1). If it is, then the minimum is at the critical point found.\n\nBut verifying convexity might require the second derivative, which could be tedious. Alternatively, use the method of Lagrange multipliers.\n\nAlternatively, maybe use the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality.\n\nWait, let's consider the two terms (a + 1/a)^2 + (b + 1/b)^2. Maybe we can apply the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality on vectors.\n\nBut I don't see an immediate way. Alternatively, consider that for any x > 0, (x + 1/x)^2 = x^2 + 2 + 1/x^2. So, as we did earlier, the expression becomes:\n\n(a^2 + b^2) + (1/a^2 + 1/b^2) + 4\n\nWe need to show that this is >= 25/2.\n\nWe already know a + b =1. Let me denote ab = t. Since a and b are positive numbers adding to 1, the maximum value of t is 1/4 (when a = b = 1/2), and t approaches 0 as one variable approaches 0.\n\nExpress a^2 + b^2 and 1/a^2 + 1/b^2 in terms of t.\n\nWe have a + b =1, ab = t.\n\na^2 + b^2 = (a + b)^2 - 2ab = 1 - 2t\n\nAlso, 1/a^2 + 1/b^2 = (a^2 + b^2)/(a^2 b^2) = (1 - 2t)/(t^2)\n\nTherefore, the expression becomes:\n\n(1 - 2t) + (1 - 2t)/(t^2) + 4 = 5 - 2t + (1 - 2t)/t\u00b2\n\nSo, need to show that 5 - 2t + (1 - 2t)/t\u00b2 >= 25/2\n\nLet me denote f(t) = 5 - 2t + (1 - 2t)/t\u00b2\n\nWe need to show that f(t) >= 25/2 for t in (0, 1/4]\n\nCompute f(t):\n\nf(t) = 5 - 2t + (1 - 2t)/t\u00b2\n\nLet me compute f(t) - 25/2:\n\n= 5 - 2t + (1 - 2t)/t\u00b2 - 25/2\n\n= (10/2 - 25/2) - 2t + (1 - 2t)/t\u00b2\n\n= (-15/2) - 2t + (1 - 2t)/t\u00b2\n\nSo, need to show that (-15/2) - 2t + (1 - 2t)/t\u00b2 >= 0\n\nMultiply both sides by 2t\u00b2 (since t > 0, this is allowed without changing inequality direction):\n\n2t\u00b2*(-15/2) + 2t\u00b2*(-2t) + 2t\u00b2*(1 - 2t)/t\u00b2 >= 0\n\nSimplify:\n\n-15t\u00b2 -4t\u00b3 + 2(1 - 2t) >= 0\n\nWhich is:\n\n-15t\u00b2 -4t\u00b3 + 2 - 4t >= 0\n\nRearranged:\n\n-4t\u00b3 -15t\u00b2 -4t + 2 >= 0\n\nMultiply both sides by -1 (which reverses the inequality):\n\n4t\u00b3 +15t\u00b2 +4t -2 <= 0\n\nSo, need to show that 4t\u00b3 +15t\u00b2 +4t -2 <= 0 for t in (0, 1/4]\n\nBut wait, when t =1/4 (the maximum value of t):\n\nCompute 4*(1/4)^3 +15*(1/4)^2 +4*(1/4) -2\n\n= 4*(1/64) +15*(1/16) +1 -2\n\n= 1/16 + 15/16 +1 -2\n\n= (1 +15)/16 + (1 -2)\n\n= 16/16 -1\n\n= 1 -1 =0\n\nSo at t=1/4, the expression is zero. For t <1/4, need to check if the expression is negative.\n\nBut since t is in (0,1/4], let's pick t=1/5 (0.2 < 0.25). Compute:\n\n4*(1/5)^3 +15*(1/5)^2 +4*(1/5) -2\n\n=4*(1/125) +15*(1/25) +4/5 -2\n\n= 4/125 + 15/25 + 4/5 -2\n\n= 0.032 + 0.6 + 0.8 -2\n\n= 0.032 + 0.6 = 0.632; 0.632 +0.8=1.432; 1.432 -2= -0.568 <0\n\nSimilarly, t approaching 0, the expression 4t\u00b3 +15t\u00b2 +4t -2 approaches -2 <0\n\nThus, the polynomial 4t\u00b3 +15t\u00b2 +4t -2 is less than or equal to 0 for t in (0,1/4], with equality only at t=1/4.\n\nTherefore, the inequality holds, which implies that f(t) >=25/2, with equality when t=1/4, i.e., when a = b =1/2.\n\nTherefore, the original expression is minimized at a = b =1/2, and the minimum value is 25/2. Hence, proved.\n\nAlternatively, another approach using the method of Lagrange multipliers.\n\nDefine the function to minimize:\n\nF(a,b) = (a + 1/a)^2 + (b + 1/b)^2\n\nSubject to the constraint G(a,b) = a + b -1 =0\n\nSet up the Lagrangian:\n\nL = (a +1/a)^2 + (b +1/b)^2 - \u03bb(a + b -1)\n\nTake partial derivatives:\n\n\u2202L/\u2202a = 2(a +1/a)(1 -1/a\u00b2) - \u03bb =0\n\n\u2202L/\u2202b = 2(b +1/b)(1 -1/b\u00b2) - \u03bb =0\n\n\u2202L/\u2202\u03bb = -(a + b -1)=0\n\nFrom the first two equations:\n\n2(a +1/a)(1 -1/a\u00b2) = \u03bb\n\n2(b +1/b)(1 -1/b\u00b2) = \u03bb\n\nThus, set them equal:\n\n2(a +1/a)(1 -1/a\u00b2) = 2(b +1/b)(1 -1/b\u00b2)\n\nDivide both sides by 2:\n\n(a +1/a)(1 -1/a\u00b2) = (b +1/b)(1 -1/b\u00b2)\n\nGiven that a + b =1, perhaps the only solution is a =b =1/2. Let's check.\n\nAssume a =b. Since a + b =1, then a =b =1/2. Then substituting into the equation:\n\n(1/2 +2)(1 -4) = (5/2)(-3) = -15/2\n\nSimilarly for b, same result. So equality holds.\n\nSuppose a \u2260b. Then we need to solve:\n\n(a +1/a)(1 -1/a\u00b2) = (b +1/b)(1 -1/b\u00b2)\n\nBut with a + b =1, this seems complicated. Maybe we can show that the function h(x) = (x +1/x)(1 -1/x\u00b2) is injective (one-to-one) in the interval (0,1), which would imply that a =b is the only solution.\n\nCompute h(x):\n\nh(x) = (x + 1/x)(1 -1/x\u00b2) = (x +1/x) - (x +1/x)/x\u00b2 = x +1/x - x/x\u00b2 -1/x\u00b3 = x +1/x -1/x -1/x\u00b3 = x -1/x\u00b3\n\nThus, h(x) = x - 1/x\u00b3\n\nSet h(a) = h(b):\n\na - 1/a\u00b3 = b -1/b\u00b3\n\nBut since a + b =1, so b =1 -a\n\nThus:\n\na -1/a\u00b3 = (1 -a) -1/(1 -a)^3\n\nBring all terms to left side:\n\na -1/a\u00b3 - (1 -a) +1/(1 -a)^3 =0\n\nSimplify:\n\na -1/a\u00b3 -1 +a +1/(1 -a)^3 =0\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n2a -1 -1/a\u00b3 +1/(1 -a)^3 =0\n\nThis is a complicated equation. Let me try plugging in a=1/2:\n\nLeft side: 2*(1/2) -1 -1/(1/2)^3 +1/(1 -1/2)^3 =1 -1 -8 +8=0. So equality holds.\n\nSuppose we let a =1/3, b=2/3:\n\nLeft side: 2*(1/3) -1 -1/(1/3)^3 +1/(2/3)^3 =2/3 -1 -27 +27/8 \u22480.666 -1 -27 +3.375\u2248-24.958 \u22600\n\nSo the equation is not satisfied. Thus, only solution is a =b =1/2. Therefore, the critical point is at a =b =1/2, which gives the minimum value of 25/2.\n\nHence, the inequality holds, with equality when a =b =1/2.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe inequality holds and the minimum value is \\boxed{\\dfrac{25}{2}}.", "deepseek_solution": "Given positive numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) such that \\(a + b = 1\\), we need to show that \\(\\left( a + \\frac{1}{a} \\right)^2 + \\left( b + \\frac{1}{b} \\right)^2 \\geq \\frac{25}{2}\\).\n\nFirst, we substitute \\(b = 1 - a\\) into the expression, leading to:\n\\[\nf(a) = \\left( a + \\frac{1}{a} \\right)^2 + \\left( (1 - a) + \\frac{1}{1 - a} \\right)^2\n\\]\n\nExpanding each term, we get:\n\\[\n\\left( a + \\frac{1}{a} \\right)^2 = a^2 + 2 + \\frac{1}{a^2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left( (1 - a) + \\frac{1}{1 - a} \\right)^2 = (1 - a)^2 + 2 + \\frac{1}{(1 - a)^2}\n\\]\n\nSumming these, the expression becomes:\n\\[\na^2 + (1 - a)^2 + 4 + \\frac{1}{a^2} + \\frac{1}{(1 - a)^2}\n\\]\n\nUsing the identity \\(a^2 + b^2 = (a + b)^2 - 2ab\\) with \\(a + b = 1\\), we get:\n\\[\na^2 + (1 - a)^2 = 1 - 2ab\n\\]\n\nThus, the expression simplifies to:\n\\[\n1 - 2ab + 4 + \\frac{1}{a^2} + \\frac{1}{(1 - a)^2} = 5 - 2ab + \\frac{1}{a^2} + \\frac{1}{(1 - a)^2}\n\\]\n\nWe need to show this is at least \\(\\frac{25}{2}\\). By setting \\(a = b = \\frac{1}{2}\\), we check:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{1}{2} + 2 \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{1}{2} + 2 \\right)^2 = \\left( \\frac{5}{2} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{5}{2} \\right)^2 = \\frac{25}{4} + \\frac{25}{4} = \\frac{25}{2}\n\\]\n\nTo confirm this is the minimum, we use the method of Lagrange multipliers or check convexity. The critical point analysis shows \\(a = b = \\frac{1}{2}\\) is the minimum. Testing other values (e.g., \\(a = \\frac{1}{3}\\), \\(b = \\frac{2}{3}\\)) results in larger values, confirming the minimum at \\(a = b = \\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\nThus, the inequality holds with equality when \\(a = b = \\frac{1}{2}\\), giving the minimum value \\(\\boxed{\\frac{25}{2}}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": ": \n\n1. **Define the function**: \n   \n   Consider the function \n   \\[\n   f(x) = \\left(x + \\frac{1}{x}\\right)^2.\n   \\]\n   \n2. **Rewrite the function**: \n   \n   We can express \\( f(x) \\) as follows:\n   \\[\n   f(x) = \\left(x + \\frac{1}{x}\\right)^2 = x^2 + 2 + \\frac{1}{x^2}.\n   \\]\n\n3. **Convexity of the function**:\n   \n   Notice that \\( x^2 \\) and \\( \\frac{1}{x^2} \\) are both convex functions for \\( x > 0 \\) (the domain of our problem). The function \\( f(x) \\) is the sum of these convex functions and the constant 2. Hence, \\( f(x) \\) is convex on \\( \\mathbb{R}_+^* \\).\n\n4. **Applying Jensen's Inequality**:\n   \n   Since \\( f(x) \\) is convex and \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are positive real numbers such that \\( a + b = 1 \\), by Jensen's Inequality for the convex function \\( f(x) \\), we have:\n   \\[\n   f(a) + f(b) \\geq 2 f\\left(\\frac{a + b}{2}\\right).\n   \\]\n   \n   Here, since \\( a + b = 1 \\), we get:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{a + b}{2} = \\frac{1}{2}.\n   \\]\n   \n5. **Evaluate the function at \\( x = \\frac{1}{2} \\)**:\n   \n   Now, we need to evaluate \\( f(x) \\) at \\( x = \\frac{1}{2} \\):\n   \\[\n   f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = \\left(\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{2}}\\right)^2 = \\left(\\frac{1}{2} + 2\\right)^2 = \\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{25}{4}.\n   \\]\n   \n6. **Final calculation**:\n   \n   Substituting \\( f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\) back into the inequality, we get:\n   \\[\n   f(a) + f(b) \\geq 2 \\cdot f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = 2 \\cdot \\frac{25}{4} = \\frac{25}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\nTherefore, we have shown that:\n\\[\n\\left(a + \\frac{1}{a}\\right)^2 + \\left(b + \\frac{1}{b}\\right)^2 \\geq \\frac{25}{2}.\n\\]\n\\(\\boxed{\\frac{25}{2}}\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Three wise men sit in chairs facing forward, such that the person in front cannot see those sitting behind. They know there are 3 white and 2 black hats. The wise men close their eyes, and hats are placed on their heads, with the remaining hats taken away. They open their eyes, and the person at the back says he does not know the color of his hat. Then the person in the middle says he also does not know the color of his hat. Does the person in front now know what color his hat is?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to figure out this puzzle. So, there are three wise men sitting in a row facing forward. The one at the back can see the two in front, the middle one can see the front person, and the front person can't see anyone. There are 3 white hats and 2 black hats total. They all close their eyes, hats are placed on them, and the remaining hats are taken away. Then they open their eyes. The person at the back says he doesn't know his hat color. Then the middle person also says he doesn't know. Now, does the front person know his hat color?\n\nHmm. Let's break this down step by step. First, let's consider the possible hat distributions. There are 3 hats used, so two hats are left. Since there are 3 white and 2 black hats, the remaining two could be both black, one black and one white, or both white. Wait, but since we have 3 white hats, if two are left, the possible combinations are:\n\nIf all three men have white hats, then there are 0 white left and 2 black left. If two are white and one is black, then 1 white and 1 black left. If one white and two black, that's impossible because there are only 2 black hats total. Wait, no. Wait, the total hats are 3 white and 2 black. So when three hats are placed on the men, the remaining two must be 3+2-3=2 hats. So possible remaining hats could be:\n\nCase 1: 0 white and 2 black (if all three men have white hats). But since there are only 2 black hats, this would require the three men to have 3 white hats, leaving 0 white and 2 black. That's possible.\n\nCase 2: 1 white and 1 black. This would happen if two men have white and one has black. Since total white used is 2, remaining white is 1, and remaining black is 1.\n\nCase 3: 2 white and 0 black. This would be if one man has white and two have black. But wait, there are only 2 black hats total, so if two men have black hats, the third must have white. So remaining hats would be 3-1=2 white hats and 2-2=0 black hats. So yes, that's possible.\n\nWait, so possible remaining hats are:\n\n- 0W 2B (if all three have white)\n\n- 1W 1B (if two W and one B on the men)\n\n- 2W 0B (if one W and two B on the men)\n\nBut the problem is that the men don't know which scenario it is. Let's think from the back person's perspective first. He can see the two in front. Let's call them A (front), B (middle), and C (back). C sees A and B. When C says he doesn't know his hat color, what does that imply?\n\nIf C saw that both A and B have black hats, then he would immediately know that his own hat must be white, because there are only two black hats total. But since C says he doesn't know, that means he doesn't see two black hats. So A and B cannot both be black. Therefore, the possible combinations that C sees are:\n\nEither both A and B are white, or one is white and the other is black.\n\nSo after C says he doesn't know, everyone knows that A and B are not both black. Now, moving on to B's turn. B hears that C doesn't know, so B knows that he and A are not both black. Now, B looks at A's hat. If B saw that A has a black hat, then B would know that his own hat must be white, because if A is black, then since C already said that they can't both be black