e black hats available), he would have known that his hat must be white.\n   \n4. Sage B, in the middle, knows that Sage C did not see two black hats:\n   - Because if Sage C had seen two black hats, he would know his own hat is white.\n   - Hence, Sage B knows there must be at least one white hat between them (A and B).\n\n5. Sage B states he does not know the color of his hat either:\n   - This informs us that Sage B did not see a black hat on A.\n       - If Sage B had seen a black hat on A, knowing that there must be at least one white hat (from the statement of Sage C not knowing), Sage B would have concluded his own hat is white.\n   \n6. Sage A, sitting in the front, realizes the implications of the statements made by Sages C and B:\n   - Since Sage B did not see a black hat on Sage A, it means the hat on A cannot be black.\n   - Therefore, Sage A deduces that his own hat must be white.\n\nConclusion: The color of the hat on the sage sitting at the front (Sage A) is white.\n\n\\[ \\boxed{\\text{White}} \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "In a soccer invitational tournament, 16 cities are represented, each sending two teams, A and B. According to the competition rules, any two teams can play at most one match against each other, and the two teams from the same city do not play against each other. After some days of competition, it was observed that except for city A's team A, all other teams have played a different number of matches. How many matches has city A's team B played? Please prove your conclusion.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out how many matches city A's team B has played in this soccer tournament. Let me try to break down the problem step by step. \n\nFirst, the setup: There are 16 cities, each with two teams, A and B. So total teams are 16*2=32 teams. The rules are that any two teams can play at most one match, and teams from the same city don't play each ot