(2\u221a2)) sin\u03b8 = a/4(1 + \u221a2 sin\u03b8)\n\nSo, E is (a/4(1 + \u221a2 cos\u03b8), a/4(1 + \u221a2 sin\u03b8))\n\nSo, BE is the distance from (0, a) to E:\n\nx-coordinate difference: a/4(1 + \u221a2 cos\u03b8) - 0 = a/4(1 + \u221a2 cos\u03b8)\n\ny-coordinate difference: a/4(1 + \u221a2 sin\u03b8) - a = a/4(1 + \u221a2 sin\u03b8) - 4a/4 = a/4(-3 + \u221a2 sin\u03b8)\n\nThus, BE^2 = [a/4(1 + \u221a2 cos\u03b8)]^2 + [a/4(-3 + \u221a2 sin\u03b8)]^2\n\nSimilarly, DE is the distance from (a, 0) to E:\n\nx-coordinate difference: a/4(1 + \u221a2 cos\u03b8) - a = a/4(1 + \u221a2 cos\u03b8) - 4a/4 = a/4(-3 + \u221a2 cos\u03b8)\n\ny-coordinate difference: a/4(1 + \u221a2 sin\u03b8) - 0 = a/4(1 + \u221a2 sin\u03b8)\n\nThus, DE^2 = [a/4(-3 + \u221a2 cos\u03b8)]^2 + [a/4(1 + \u221a2 sin\u03b8)]^2\n\nLet me compute BE^2 + DE^2:\n\nFirst, factor out (a/4)^2 from both terms:\n\nBE^2 + DE^2 = (a/4)^2 [ (1 + \u221a2 cos\u03b8)^2 + (-3 + \u221a2 sin\u03b8)^2 + (-3 + \u221a2 cos\u03b8)^2 + (1 + \u221a2 sin\u03b8)^2 ]\n\nLet me compute the terms inside:\n\nFirst, expand (1 + \u221a2 cos\u03b8)^2 = 1 + 2\u221a2 cos\u03b8 + 2 cos\u00b2\u03b8\n\nSecond, (-3 + \u221a2 sin\u03b8)^2 = 9 - 6\u221a2 sin\u03b8 + 2 sin\u00b2\u03b8\n\nThird, (-3 + \u221a2 cos\u03b8)^2 = 9 - 6\u221a2 cos\u03b8 + 2 cos\u00b2\u03b8\n\nFourth, (1 + \u221a2 sin\u03b8)^2 = 1 + 2\u221a2 sin\u03b8 + 2 sin\u00b2\u03b8\n\nAdding all these together:\n\n1 + 2\u221a2 cos\u03b8 + 2 cos\u00b2\u03b8 + 9 - 6\u221a2 sin\u03b8 + 2 sin\u00b2\u03b8 + 9 - 6\u221a2 cos\u03b8 + 2 cos\u00b2\u03b8 + 1 + 2\u221a2 sin\u03b8 + 2 sin\u00b2\u03b8\n\nCombine like terms:\n\nConstants: 1 + 9 + 9 + 1 = 20\n\nTerms with cos\u03b8: 2\u221a2 cos\u03b8 -6\u221a2 cos\u03b8 = (-4\u221a2 cos\u03b8)\n\nTerms with sin\u03b8: -6\u221a2 sin\u03b8 + 2\u221a2 sin\u03b8 = (-4\u221a2 sin\u03b8)\n\nTerms with cos\u00b2\u03b8: 2 cos\u00b2\u03b8 + 2 cos\u00b2\u03b8 = 4 cos\u00b2\u03b8\n\nTerms with sin\u00b2\u03b8: 2 sin\u00b2\u03b8 + 2 sin\u00b2\u03b8 = 4 sin\u00b2\u03b8\n\nSo total:\n\n20 -4\u221a2 (cos\u03b8 + sin\u03b8) +4(cos\u00b2\u03b8 + sin\u00b2\u03b8)\n\nBut cos\u00b2\u03b8 + sin\u00b2\u03b8 = 1, so:\n\n20 -4\u221a2 (cos\u03b8 + sin\u03b8) +4(1) = 24 -4\u221a2 (cos\u03b8 + sin\u03b8)\n\nTherefore, BE^2 + DE^2 = (a\u00b2/16)(24 -4\u221a2 (cos\u03b8 + sin\u03b8)) = (a\u00b2/16)(24 -4\u221a2 (cos\u03b8 + sin\u03b8)) = (a\u00b2/4)(6 - \u221a2 (cos\u03b8 + sin\u03b8))\n\nNow, we need to compute BE + DE. However, BE + DE is not the same as sqrt(BE^2 + DE^2). Instead, it's the sum of two square roots. Hmm, this might not be straightforward. Maybe using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality? But we need BE + DE \u2265 AC.\n\nFirst, AC is the distance from A to C. Since C is (0,0) and A is (a/2 + (a/\u221a2) cos\u03b8, a/2 + (a/\u221a2) sin\u03b8), then AC^2 = [a/2 + (a/\u221a2) cos\u03b8]^2 + [a/2 + (a/\u221a2) sin\u03b8]^2\n\nCompute AC^2:\n\n= (a^2/4 + a^2/(\u221a2) cos\u03b8 + (a\u00b2/2) cos\u00b2\u03b8) + (a^2/4 + a^2/(\u221a2) sin\u03b8 + (a\u00b2/2) sin\u00b2\u03b8)\n\n= a\u00b2/4 + a\u00b2/4 + (a\u00b2/\u221a2)(cos\u03b8 + sin\u03b8) + (a\u00b2/2)(cos\u00b2\u03b8 + sin\u00b2\u03b8)\n\n= a\u00b2/2 + (a\u00b2/\u221a2)(cos\u03b8 + sin\u03b8) + (a\u00b2/2)(1)\n\n= a\u00b2/2 + a\u00b2/2 + (a\u00b2/\u221a2)(cos\u03b8 + sin\u03b8)\n\n= a\u00b2 + (a\u00b2/\u221a2)(cos\u03b8 + sin\u03b8)\n\nTherefore, AC = a sqrt(1 + (1/\u221a2)(cos\u03b8 + sin\u03b8))\n\nWait, actually, AC^2 = a\u00b2 [1 + (1/\u221a2)(cos\u03b8 + sin\u03b8)]\n\nSo, AC = a sqrt(1 + (1/\u221a2)(cos\u03b8 + sin\u03b8))\n\nHmm, so we need to show that BE + DE \u2265 AC. Let's see. So BE + DE \u2265 AC.\n\nBut BE and DE are expressed in terms of \u03b8 as well. Maybe we can relate the expressions. Alternatively, perhaps instead of coordinates, this approach is getting too messy. Let me think of another method.\n\nAnother idea: Since E is the midpoint of AC, perhaps use the median properties. In triangle ABC, the length of the median can be related to the sides. B