s(4t) + cos^2(4t)).\n\nSo \u222b cos^4(2t) dt = (1/4) \u222b [1 + 2 cos(4t) + cos^2(4t)] dt.\n\nCompute each part:\n\n\u222b 1 dt = t.\n\n\u222b 2 cos(4t) dt = 2*(1/4) sin(4t) = (1/2) sin(4t).\n\n\u222b cos^2(4t) dt = \u222b (1 + cos(8t))/2 dt = (1/2)t + (1/16) sin(8t).\n\nPutting it all together:\n\n\u222b cos^4(2t) dt = (1/4)[ t + (1/2) sin(4t) + (1/2)t + (1/16) sin(8t) ] + C = (1/4)[ (3/2)t + (1/2) sin(4t) + (1/16) sin(8t) ] + C.\n\nEvaluating from 0 to \u03c0/2:\n\nAt t = \u03c0/2:\n\n(3/2)*(\u03c0/2) + (1/2) sin(2\u03c0) + (1/16) sin(4\u03c0) = (3\u03c0/4) + 0 + 0 = 3\u03c0/4.\n\nAt t = 0:\n\nAll terms are 0. So the integral from 0 to \u03c0/2 is (1/4)*(3\u03c0/4 - 0) = (3\u03c0)/16.\n\nTherefore, the fifth term is (3\u03c0)/16.\n\nPutting all the terms together:\n\nOriginal integral of sin^8(t) dt from 0 to \u03c0/2 is (1/16)[\u03c0/2 + 0 + (3/2)\u03c0 + 0 + (3\u03c0)/16].\n\nWait, let me verify:\n\nFirst term: (1/16)*(\u03c0/2)\n\nThird term: (1/16)*(3/2 \u03c0)\n\nFifth term: (1/16)*(3\u03c0/16)\n\nWait, no. Wait, the entire integral is (1/16)[ first term integral + second term integral + ...]. So:\n\nFirst term: 1/16 * \u03c0/2\n\nSecond term: 1/16 * 0\n\nThird term: 1/16 * 3/2 \u03c0\n\nFourth term: 1/16 * 0\n\nFifth term: 1/16 * 3\u03c0/16\n\nWait, no. Wait, the coefficients:\n\nWait, the integral of sin^8(t) dt is (1/16) times [ integral of 1 -4 cos(2t) +6 cos^2(2t) -4 cos^3(2t) + cos^4(2t) dt ].\n\nSo:\n\nIntegral of 1 dt = \u03c0/2.\n\nIntegral of -4 cos(2t) dt = -4*(0) = 0.\n\nIntegral of 6 cos^2(2t) dt = 6*(\u03c0/4) = 3\u03c0/2.\n\nIntegral of -4 cos^3(2t) dt = -4*(0) = 0.\n\nIntegral of cos^4(2t) dt = 3\u03c0/16.\n\nSo summing these up:\n\n\u03c0/2 + 0 + 3\u03c0/2 + 0 + 3\u03c0/16.\n\nLet me compute this:\n\nFirst, \u03c0/2 + 3\u03c0/2 = 2\u03c0.\n\nThen, 2\u03c0 + 3\u03c0/16 = (32\u03c0/16 + 3\u03c0/16) = 35\u03c0/16.\n\nTherefore, the integral of sin^8(t) dt from 0 to \u03c0/2 is (1/16)*(35\u03c0/16) = 35\u03c0/256.\n\nWait, wait, hold on. Wait, 